            ['Current VA funding fee', 'For purchase loans (rates effective April 7, 2023): <strong>first use</strong> is 2.15% with less than 5% down, 1.5% with 5% or more, and 1.25% with 10% or more. <strong>After first use</strong> it is 3.3% with less than 5% down, then the same 1.5% and 1.25%. Veterans receiving VA disability compensation, surviving spouses receiving DIC and Purple Heart recipients on active duty are exempt.'],
            ['No PMI, but taxes still count', 'VA loans have no monthly mortgage insurance, which is why they often beat FHA and low-down-payment conventional loans. Property taxes and homeowners insurance still apply - and in the Houston area they can be a large share of the payment.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Do I have to pay the VA funding fee in cash?', 'No. Most borrowers finance it into the loan, which is what this calculator assumes. You can also pay it at closing or negotiate for the seller to cover it as part of concessions.'],
            ['Is there a VA loan limit?', 'Veterans with full entitlement have no VA loan limit, though lenders still require you to qualify for the payment. If you have a VA loan already outstanding, remaining entitlement can limit a no-down-payment purchase.'],
            ['Does Texas have its own veteran home loan?', 'Yes. The Texas Veterans Land Board offers home loans for eligible Texas veterans, which can be paired with VA or other financing. Ask a participating lender how the rates compare.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['first-time-home-buyer-texas', 'closing-costs-in-texas', 'down-payment-assistance-texas'],
    ],
    'down-payment-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Down Payment Calculator',
        'short' => 'How much to put down, and how long to save it.',
        'title' => 'Down Payment Calculator: How Much Do I Need?',
        'description' => 'See the down payment you need for conventional, FHA, VA and USDA loans, plus closing costs, and how many months it will take you to save it.',
        'icon' => 'cash',
        'group' => 'Buying',
        'intro' => 'Enter a target price to see the minimum down payment for each major loan type, the cash you need with closing costs, and how long it will take to save based on what you have now and what you can set aside each month.',
        'how' => [
            ['Minimum down payments by loan type', 'Common minimums: <strong>conventional</strong> 3% for eligible first-time buyers (5% is typical otherwise, 20% avoids PMI), <strong>FHA</strong> 3.5% with a 580+ credit score, and <strong>VA</strong> and <strong>USDA</strong> 0% for eligible borrowers and properties. A bigger down payment lowers your payment and can remove mortgage insurance.'],
            ['Don\'t forget closing costs', 'Texas buyers often pay roughly 2%-5% of the price in closing costs and prepaids on top of the down payment. Down payment assistance programs and seller credits can cover part of both.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Do I really need 20% down?', 'No. 20% down avoids PMI on a conventional loan, but many buyers put down 3%-10% and accept PMI, or use FHA, VA or USDA loans. Compare the monthly cost of PMI with how long it would take to save the extra cash.'],
            ['Can I use gift money for a down payment?', 'Usually yes. Conventional, FHA and VA loans allow documented gifts from family, and some allow gifts from employers or charities. Your lender will need a signed gift letter and bank records.'],
            ['Where should I keep down payment savings?', 'Somewhere safe and liquid, such as a high-yield savings account, money market fund or short-term CDs. The return field in this calculator lets you model the interest.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['down-payment-assistance-texas', 'first-time-home-buyer-texas', 'fha-loan-requirements-texas'],
    ],
    'rent-affordability-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Rent Affordability Calculator',
        'short' => 'How much rent can you afford on your income?',
        'title' => 'Rent Affordability Calculator: What Can I Afford?',
        'description' => 'How much rent can you afford? See your budget by the 30% rule, the 50/30/20 budget and the income landlords often require from your pay.',
        'icon' => 'home',
        'group' => 'Buying',
        'intro' => 'Find a rent you can comfortably afford. Enter your income and monthly debts to see your budget three ways: the classic 30% rule, a 50/30/20 budget that accounts for your debts, and the "3x the rent" income test many landlords use.',
        'how' => [
            ['Three ways to set a rent budget', 'The <strong>30% rule</strong> caps rent at 30% of gross income. The <strong>50/30/20 budget</strong> puts needs (rent, utilities, minimum debt payments, groceries) at 50% of take-home pay. Many landlords also want gross monthly income of about <strong>three times the rent</strong>. The most conservative of the three is a comfortable target.'],
            ['Budget for utilities', 'In Houston, summer electricity bills can be high, and many apartments bill water, trash and pest control separately. Enter an estimate so the calculator includes them in your housing cost.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['How much rent can I afford on $60,000 a year?', 'By the 30% rule, $60,000 gross supports about $1,500 a month in rent including utilities. A landlord using the 3x rule would approve up to about $1,667. If you carry car or student loan payments, your comfortable number is lower.'],
            ['Is the 30% rule based on gross or net income?', 'Gross (before taxes). The 50/30/20 method uses take-home pay instead, which is why this calculator asks for both.'],
            ['Should I rent or buy?', 'It depends on how long you will stay, prices and rates. Compare both with our rent vs. buy calculator.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['cost-of-living-in-houston', 'moving-to-houston', 'best-neighborhoods-in-houston'],
    ],
    'home-equity-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'HELOC & Home Equity Calculator',
        'short' => 'How much you can borrow, with the Texas 80% rule.',
        'title' => 'HELOC & Home Equity Calculator (Texas 80% Rule)',
        'description' => 'See how much home equity you can borrow with a HELOC or home equity loan under the Texas 80% limit, plus interest-only and repayment payments.',
        'icon' => 'percent',
        'group' => 'Homeownership',
        'intro' => 'Find out how much you can borrow against your home and what it will cost each month. The calculator applies a combined loan-to-value limit - 80% by default, the Texas cap for home equity borrowing on a homestead - then estimates HELOC payments during the draw and repayment periods.',
        'how' => [
            ['The Texas 80% rule', 'The Texas Constitution (Article XVI, Section 50(a)(6)) generally limits home equity borrowing on a homestead so that <strong>all loans secured by the home total no more than 80% of its fair market value</strong>. Texas home equity loans also come with extra consumer protections, such as a waiting period before closing and limits on how often you can take one. Investment properties are not subject to the homestead rules.'],
            ['Draw period vs. repayment', 'Most HELOCs start with a draw period (often 10 years) where you can pay interest only, then a repayment period where the balance is amortized. The jump between the two payments surprises many borrowers, so the calculator shows both.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['How much equity do I have?', 'Equity is your home\'s market value minus what you owe. The amount you can borrow is smaller: lenders limit total debt to a percentage of value, which in Texas is generally 80% for a homestead.'],
            ['HELOC or cash-out refinance?', 'A HELOC keeps your existing first mortgage, which is valuable if your current rate is low. A cash-out refinance replaces it with one new loan. Compare both with our cash-out refinance calculator.'],
            ['Are HELOC rates fixed?', 'Usually not. Most HELOCs have a variable rate tied to the prime rate, so the payment can change. Some lenders let you lock part of the balance at a fixed rate.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['texas-homestead-exemption', 'houston-home-maintenance-checklist', 'homeowners-insurance-texas'],
    ],
    'cash-out-refinance-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Cash-Out Refinance Calculator',
        'short' => 'Cash available, new payment and the Texas 80% cap.',
        'title' => 'Cash-Out Refinance Calculator (Texas Rules)',
        'description' => 'See how much cash you can take out with a refinance under the Texas 80% limit, your new payment, and how it compares with your current mortgage.',
        'icon' => 'refresh',
        'group' => 'Homeownership',
        'intro' => 'A cash-out refinance replaces your mortgage with a larger loan and pays you the difference. Enter your home value, current loan and the new terms to see the maximum cash available, the new payment and the change from today.',
        'how' => [
            ['How much cash you can take out', 'The new loan can be no more than the loan-to-value limit times your home\'s value. In Texas, a cash-out refinance of a homestead is generally capped at <strong>80% of fair market value</strong>, and conventional and FHA cash-out loans use 80% as well. Your cash in hand is the new loan minus your payoff and closing costs.'],
            ['Watch the rate trade-off', 'If your current rate is lower than today\'s rates, a cash-out refinance raises the cost of the money you already owe, not just the new cash. In that case a HELOC or home equity loan can be cheaper - compare with our HELOC calculator.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Can I do a cash-out refinance on a rental in Texas?', 'Yes. The Texas homestead limits apply to your primary residence. Investment property cash-out loans follow lender and program rules instead, often around 70%-75% loan-to-value.'],
            ['Can I convert a Texas cash-out loan back to a regular refinance?', 'Texas law allows a later refinance of a home equity loan into a non-equity loan in some cases if specific conditions are met, including the 80% limit. Ask your lender whether you qualify.'],
            ['How long does a Texas cash-out refinance take?', 'Texas home equity loans have required waiting periods, including time after you receive the required notice before closing and a rescission period after closing, so plan for a longer timeline than a standard refinance.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['texas-homestead-exemption', 'how-to-analyze-a-rental-property', 'brrrr-method-explained'],
    ],
    'capital-gains-tax-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Home Sale Capital Gains Calculator',
        'short' => 'Gain, the $250k/$500k exclusion and estimated tax.',
        'title' => 'Capital Gains Tax Calculator for a Home Sale',
        'description' => 'Estimate capital gains tax on a home sale: adjusted basis, the $250,000/$500,000 exclusion, 2026 federal rates and the 3.8% NIIT.',
        'icon' => 'tag',
        'group' => 'Selling',
        'intro' => 'Most homeowners owe nothing when they sell a primary residence, thanks to the Section 121 exclusion. Enter your numbers to see your adjusted basis, gain, how much the exclusion shelters, and an estimate of any federal tax using 2026 rates. Texas has no state income tax.',
        'how' => [
            ['How the gain is calculated', 'Gain = sale price minus selling costs minus your <strong>adjusted basis</strong> (purchase price, certain purchase closing costs and capital improvements, less any depreciation you claimed). If you owned and lived in the home for at least 2 of the last 5 years, you can exclude up to <strong>$250,000</strong> of gain, or <strong>$500,000</strong> if married filing jointly.'],
            ['2026 long-term capital gains rates', 'For 2026 (IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32), long-term gains are taxed at 0% up to $49,450 of taxable income for single filers ($98,900 married filing jointly), 15% up to $545,500 ($613,700 joint), and 20% above. A 3.8% net investment income tax may apply above $200,000 of modified AGI ($250,000 joint). Depreciation claimed after May 6, 1997 cannot be excluded and is taxed at up to 25%.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Do I pay capital gains tax if I sell my house in Texas?', 'Texas has no state income tax, so only federal tax can apply. If you meet the ownership and use tests, the first $250,000 of gain ($500,000 for most married couples) is excluded, so many sellers owe nothing.'],
            ['What if I lived there less than two years?', 'You may qualify for a partial exclusion if you moved for a job change, health reasons or certain unforeseen circumstances. This calculator does not compute partial exclusions - see IRS Publication 523 or a CPA.'],
            ['What counts as a capital improvement?', 'Improvements that add value, extend the home\'s life or adapt it to new uses - a new roof, HVAC system, addition, remodel or foundation repair. Routine repairs and maintenance do not count. Keep receipts for as long as you own the home.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['capital-gains-tax-on-home-sale', 'sell-house-by-owner-texas', '1031-exchange-texas'],
    ],
    'cap-rate-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Cap Rate Calculator',
        'short' => 'NOI, cap rate and value at a target cap rate.',
        'title' => 'Cap Rate Calculator for Rental Property',
        'description' => 'Free cap rate calculator: find net operating income and capitalization rate for any rental, and the value or offer price at your target cap rate.',
        'icon' => 'chart',
        'group' => 'Investing',
        'intro' => 'The capitalization rate is the unlevered yield of a property: net operating income divided by price. Enter the rent and expenses to get NOI and cap rate, then set a target cap rate to see what the property is worth to you.',
        'how' => [
            ['The cap rate formula', '<strong>Cap rate = NOI / price</strong>. NOI is gross rent plus other income, minus vacancy and operating expenses (taxes, insurance, management, repairs, reserves, HOA, owner-paid utilities). It excludes mortgage payments, depreciation and income taxes, which is what makes cap rates comparable across properties.'],
            ['Valuing a property', 'Rearranged, <strong>value = NOI / cap rate</strong>. If similar properties trade at a 6.5% cap rate and a building produces $26,000 of NOI, it is worth about $400,000. Small changes in cap rate move value a lot, so make sure the expenses in your NOI are realistic.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['What is a good cap rate?', 'There is no single number. Cap rates reflect risk and growth: newer properties in strong areas trade at lower cap rates, while older or riskier properties need higher ones. Compare against recent sales of similar properties nearby.'],
            ['Cap rate vs. cash-on-cash return?', 'Cap rate ignores financing. Cash-on-cash return measures annual cash flow after the mortgage divided by the cash you invested. Use our rental property calculator for both.'],
            ['Should property taxes be in NOI?', 'Yes. Taxes are an operating expense and, in Texas, often the largest one. Use the tax the property will pay after a sale reassessment, not the seller\'s possibly lower current bill.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['how-to-analyze-a-rental-property', 'brrrr-method-explained', '1031-exchange-texas'],
    ],
    'house-flipping-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'House Flipping Calculator',
        'short' => '70% rule offer, profit and ROI on a flip.',
        'title' => 'House Flipping Calculator: Profit & 70% Rule',
        'description' => 'Run a fix-and-flip: maximum offer by the 70% rule, rehab, holding, financing and selling costs, plus net profit, ROI and annualized return.',
        'icon' => 'wrench',
        'group' => 'Investing',
        'intro' => 'Most flips that lose money were bought at the wrong price. Enter the after-repair value, rehab budget and your costs to see the maximum offer under the 70% rule, your all-in cost, projected profit and return on cash.',
        'how' => [
            ['The 70% rule', 'A common screen: <strong>maximum offer = ARV x 70% - repair costs</strong>. The 30% cushion is meant to cover closing, holding, financing and selling costs plus profit. It is a starting point - in higher-priced areas or with expensive financing, run the full numbers below.'],
            ['Costs flippers forget', 'Holding costs (loan interest, taxes, insurance, utilities) add up every month the project runs. Selling costs - commissions, title policy, concessions - can take 7%-10% of the sale price. Always add a contingency to the rehab budget.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['How do I estimate ARV?', 'After-repair value comes from recent sales of renovated homes similar in size, age and location - ideally within the last six months and close by. Our price per square foot calculator helps compare comps.'],
            ['What profit should a flip make?', 'Many investors want at least 10%-15% of ARV or a meaningful minimum dollar profit to justify the risk. Factor in your time and the chance the project runs long.'],
            ['Do I need to disclose repairs when I sell?', 'Yes. Texas sellers must deliver the seller\'s disclosure notice, and flippers should disclose known defects and the work performed. Keep permits and invoices for buyers.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['brrrr-method-explained', 'how-to-analyze-a-rental-property', 'selling-a-house-as-is-texas'],
    ],
    '1031-exchange-calculator' => [
        'name' => '1031 Exchange Calculator',
        'short' => '45/180-day deadlines, boot and deferred tax.',
        'title' => '1031 Exchange Calculator: Deadlines & Boot',
        'description' => 'Find your 1031 exchange 45-day and 180-day deadlines from your closing date, and estimate realized gain, taxable boot and the tax you can defer.',
        'icon' => 'refresh',
        'group' => 'Investing',
        'intro' => 'A 1031 exchange lets you defer tax when you sell investment real estate and buy more. Enter your sale closing date to get the identification and exchange deadlines, then your numbers to estimate how much gain you defer and whether any boot becomes taxable.',
        'how' => [
            ['The two deadlines', 'You have <strong>45 calendar days</strong> after the sale closes to identify replacement property in writing and <strong>180 calendar days</strong> to close on it - or until your tax return due date for that year, if earlier, unless you file an extension. The deadlines are not extended for weekends or holidays.'],
            ['Boot', 'To defer all gain, buy replacement property of equal or greater value, reinvest all net proceeds and replace the debt you paid off (with new debt or added cash). Cash you keep and debt reduction not replaced are <strong>boot</strong>, which is taxable up to the amount of your gain.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Can I do a 1031 exchange on my house?', 'No. 1031 exchanges are for real property held for investment or business use. Your primary residence may instead qualify for the $250,000/$500,000 home sale exclusion.'],
            ['Do I need a qualified intermediary?', 'Yes, for a standard delayed exchange. If you receive the sale proceeds yourself, the exchange fails. The intermediary must be engaged before the sale closes.'],
            ['Does Texas tax the deferred gain?', 'Texas has no state income tax, so there is no state tax to defer or recapture. Federal capital gains tax, depreciation recapture and possibly the 3.8% NIIT are what the exchange defers.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['1031-exchange-texas', 'how-to-analyze-a-rental-property', 'capital-gains-tax-on-home-sale'],
    ],
    'price-per-square-foot-calculator' => [
        'name' => 'Price per Square Foot & Comps Calculator',
        'short' => 'Compare comps and check your appraisal for a protest.',
        'title' => 'Price per Square Foot Calculator (Comps)',
        'description' => 'Calculate price per square foot, compare up to six sales, and see the value they suggest for your home - handy for offers and tax protests.',
        'icon' => 'scale',
        'group' => 'Homeownership',
        'intro' => 'Enter your home\'s size and a list of comparable sales. The calculator finds each comp\'s price per square foot, the median, and the value it implies for your home - then compares it with a list price or your appraisal district value to support an offer or a property tax protest.',
        'how' => [
            ['Why use the median', 'One unusual sale can skew an average. The median price per square foot of several similar, recent, nearby sales is a more reliable benchmark. Choose comps with similar age, size, condition and lot, and adjust for big differences like a pool or updated kitchen.'],
            ['Using it for a tax protest', 'If the median of good comps implies a value below your appraisal district value, that is evidence for a protest. In Harris County, include the comps\' addresses, sale dates and photos, and the condition issues that set your home apart. Texas is a non-disclosure state, so sale prices often come from an agent or your appraisal district\'s evidence packet.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['How do I find comparable sales in Texas?', 'Because Texas does not require sale prices to be disclosed publicly, the best sources are a real estate agent\'s MLS search, listing sites\' sold data, and the evidence your appraisal district must provide before a protest hearing.'],
            ['Should I use living area or total square feet?', 'Use heated living area for every property, as reported by the appraisal district, so the comparison is consistent. Garages, porches and patios are not counted.'],
            ['How many comps do I need?', 'Three to six good comps is typical. More, closer and more recent comps make the result more convincing.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['protest-property-taxes-harris-county', 'houston-property-tax-rate', 'sell-house-by-owner-texas'],
    ],
    'moving-checklist' => [
        'name' => 'Moving Checklist',
        'short' => 'Week-by-week checklist with Texas to-dos. Saves progress.',
        'title' => 'Moving Checklist: Week-by-Week (Texas)',
        'description' => 'An interactive week-by-week moving checklist with Texas to-dos: electricity, homestead exemption, driver license and car registration.',
        'icon' => 'box',
        'group' => 'Buying',
        'intro' => 'Work through your move from eight weeks out to the first month in your new home. The list includes Texas-specific tasks new residents and new homeowners often miss. Your check marks save in this browser, and you can print it.',
        'how' => [
            ['Texas tasks for new residents', 'Texas generally gives new residents <strong>30 days to register a vehicle</strong> and <strong>90 days to get a Texas driver license</strong>. In deregulated areas like Houston you choose your electricity retailer (compare plans on the state\'s Power to Choose site), and new homeowners should file the homestead exemption with the county appraisal district.'],
            ['Protect your stuff', 'Photograph valuables before packing, keep important documents and medications with you, and check whether your mover\'s liability coverage is full value or only the minimum per-pound rate.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['How far ahead should I start planning a move?', 'Six to eight weeks is ideal, especially in summer when movers book up. Book movers or a truck as soon as you have a date.'],
            ['How do I set up electricity in Houston?', 'Houston is in a deregulated market, so you pick a retail electric provider. Compare plans by the price at your expected usage and read the Electricity Facts Label before signing.'],
            ['When should I file my homestead exemption?', 'You can apply in the year you buy the home. File with your county appraisal district (HCAD in Harris County) - the usual deadline is April 30, with late filing allowed in many cases.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['moving-to-houston', 'cost-of-living-in-houston', 'texas-homestead-exemption'],
    ],
    'home-inventory-checklist' => [
        'name' => 'Home Inventory Checklist',
        'short' => 'Room-by-room inventory for insurance. Exports to CSV.',
        'title' => 'Home Inventory Checklist for Insurance',
        'description' => 'Build a room-by-room home inventory for insurance with values and serial numbers. Totals by room, saves in your browser, and exports to CSV.',
        'icon' => 'list',
        'group' => 'Insurance & Claims',
        'intro' => 'After a fire, flood or burglary, insurers ask you to list everything you lost - from memory. Build that list now. Add items room by room with value, age and serial numbers, then export a spreadsheet to store with your policy. Everything stays in your browser.',
        'how' => [
            ['Why a home inventory matters', 'A contents claim is only as good as your list. An inventory helps you choose the right amount of personal property coverage, speeds up a claim, and supports the replacement cost of items you would otherwise forget. Pair it with photos or a video walk-through of every room.'],
            ['What to record', 'For each item: description, brand and model, serial number, when you bought it, what it would cost to replace today, and quantity. Prioritize electronics, appliances, furniture, jewelry, tools and collections, and keep receipts where you can.'],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['Where should I keep my home inventory?', 'Somewhere that will survive a disaster at home: cloud storage, email it to yourself, or keep a copy with a relative. Export the CSV after every update.'],
            ['Is my inventory sent to this website?', 'No. It is saved only in this browser on this device. Clearing your browser data deletes it, so export a CSV backup regularly.'],
            ['Do I list replacement cost or what I paid?', 'Record both if you can. Replacement cost policies pay what it costs to replace the item today (often after you replace it), while actual cash value policies deduct depreciation. See our depreciation calculator.'],
        ],
        'posts' => ['what-to-do-after-a-house-fire', 'how-to-file-a-home-insurance-claim', 'loss-of-use-coverage'],
    ],
];
